gm-method (a plain-appendChild'd sibling DOM node placed INSIDE an element owned by a webjsx/virtual-DOM diff call is silently removed on the diff's first run, not merely on the next render after the appendChild -- the diff owns and reconciles that parent's FULL child list against its vnode tree, treating any out-of-band child as extraneous and pruning it): witnessed adding a persistent third-party UI-library root node (a window-manager's root container) as a child of an element that a framework's applyDiff(el,[vnode]) call already owns -- the node existed immediately after appendChild but vanished the instant the owning render() function's next diff pass ran, with zero thrown error (diff libraries generally don't warn on "unexpected child removed", they just reconcile silently). Fix: never append a manually-managed persistent node as a CHILD of a diff-owned element; make it a SIBLING under a shared ancestor the diff library does not touch, and mirror any visibility/display state the diff-owned sibling has via direct style/class writes instead of relying on the diff to carry it. Diagnosed via live browser evaluate (grep the constructed node's ancestor chain immediately post-construction vs. after one render cycle) rather than guessing -- the node-exists-then-vanishes signature is the tell that a diff pass, not a logic bug, ate it.